<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" elementFormDefault="qualified" attributeFormDefault="unqualified">
	<xs:annotation>
		<xs:documentation>
      Purpose
      -------
      Request payload for the customer search operation findCustomersB. The XML travels in the
      form parameter "XmlInput". The service does not validate against this schema; it reads
      elements by LOCAL NAME, case-insensitively, in any order, and ignores anything it does not
      recognise. This schema documents the structure the service reads and the rules it applies
      (verified against the loyalty-app service generation, 2026-10).

      Namespace note
      --------------
      This schema has no target namespace (earlier revisions declared one). The service matches
      element local names only, so a document carrying a namespace declaration is read
      identically; callers in production send the request without one.

      Envelope
      --------
      Root REQUEST (the root name itself is not checked) containing one CRITERIA block.
      A request with no CRITERIA block, or with none of the "counting" filters below, returns E105.

      Filters that count toward "at least one filter" (else E105)
      ------------------------------------------------------------
      CUSTOMER_ID, SYSTEM_REFERENCE, SYSTEM_REFERENCE_ID, FIRST_NAME, LAST_NAME, CITY, ZIP_CODE,
      EMAIL_ID, PHONE_NUMBER.
      NOT counted: PROVINCE, STATE, ZIP4, STORE_ID, RECORDS_LIMIT, REQUEST_DATE. A request carrying
      only those returns E105.

      Search patterns (how the filters combine)
      -----------------------------------------
      1. Cross-reference lookup: SYSTEM_REFERENCE + SYSTEM_REFERENCE_ID. Exact match on the pair;
         returns the single most recently created ENABLED customer holding that identifier.
         PHONE_NUMBER, EMAIL_ID or CUSTOMER_ID supplied alongside must ALSO match (logical AND).
         FIRST_NAME, LAST_NAME, CITY, PROVINCE and ZIP_CODE supplied alongside do not narrow a
         cross-reference hit. The result never contains a customer without the identifier.
      2. Phone lookup: PHONE_NUMBER (used when CITY and PROVINCE are absent). FIRST_NAME /
         LAST_NAME, when present, must match exactly. All matches are returned (up to the cap).
      3. E-mail lookup: EMAIL_ID (used when CITY, PROVINCE and PHONE_NUMBER are absent).
         "Starts-with" match on the trimmed value. FIRST_NAME / LAST_NAME must match exactly.
      4. Customer id lookup: CUSTOMER_ID. Exact match. When combined with a phone, e-mail or ZIP
         search it acts as an additional exact filter on that result.
      5. Name lookup: FIRST_NAME and/or LAST_NAME alone (general customer search).
      6. Postal lookup: ZIP_CODE (optionally ZIP4, FIRST_NAME, LAST_NAME). Names are "contains"
         matches in this path. See ZipType for the length rules.
      7. Address lookup: CITY and/or PROVINCE. When either is present the PHONE_NUMBER and
         EMAIL_ID lookups are NOT performed.
      Precedence when several lookups are supplied: address (CITY / PROVINCE), then phone, then
      e-mail, then cross-reference-only.

      Result size
      -----------
      Only ENABLED customers are returned. At most 50 customers are returned; there is no paging
      and no total count. RECORDS_LIMIT is read but not applied in the loyalty-app service
      generation.

      Error codes: see the response schema.
    </xs:documentation>
	</xs:annotation>
	<!-- ===== Simple types ===== -->
	<xs:simpleType name="EmailType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>
        E-mail filter. The service performs NO format validation: the trimmed value is used as a
        "starts-with" match against stored e-mail addresses, so a partial address is accepted and
        can return several customers. (The pattern previously published here used ^ and $ anchors,
        which are literal characters in XML Schema regular expressions and therefore rejected every
        real e-mail address; it has been removed.)
      </xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:maxLength value="254"/>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="PhoneType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>
        Phone filter. The value is passed to the phone lookup as supplied (the loyalty-app service
        generation does NOT split off an area code). Send digits only, e.g. "5555550100".
      </xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:maxLength value="32"/>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="ZipType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>
        ZIP / postal code filter. Behaviour depends on the length of the value:
          5        exact match on the 5-character base code
          6, 7, 8  first 5 characters exact + the remainder as a "starts-with" match on the extension
          9        first 5 characters exact + last 4 exact on the extension
          other    (shorter than 5 or longer than 9) no postal search is run; unless another lookup
                   applies, the result is "not found"
        When ZIP4 is also supplied: first 5 characters of ZIP_CODE exact + ZIP4 exact, regardless of
        the ZIP_CODE length.
      </xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string"/>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="Zip4Type">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>
        ZIP+4 extension. Only consulted together with ZIP_CODE (exact match on the extension).
        ZIP4 on its own is not a filter and does not count toward the "at least one filter" rule.
      </xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string"/>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SystemReferenceCode">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>
        Name of the external-system identifier type to search on. OPEN string: the service accepts
        any identifier type configured for the deployment and does not check the value against a
        list (an unknown type simply finds nothing). Common values: POS_ID, LOYALTY_ID.
        A small set of legacy brand-specific identifier types additionally selects brand-aware
        solicitation flags in the response.
      </xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string"/>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RecordsLimit">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>
        Requested maximum number of records. Read and parsed as an integer (a non-numeric value is
        silently ignored) but NOT applied by the loyalty-app service generation: the result is always
        capped at 50 customers. Kept for compatibility.
      </xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string"/>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<!-- ===== CRITERIA type ===== -->
	<xs:complexType name="CriteriaType">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>
        Search filters. Elements may appear in any order; all are optional individually, but at
        least one "counting" filter (see the schema header) must carry a value or the service
        returns E105. An element that is present but empty is treated as absent.
      </xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:all>
			<xs:element name="CUSTOMER_ID" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Customer identifier, exact match. Returns that customer (if enabled) or "not found".</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SYSTEM_REFERENCE" type="SystemReferenceCode" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>
            External-system identifier type (e.g. POS_ID, LOYALTY_ID). Normally paired with
            SYSTEM_REFERENCE_ID. Supplied WITHOUT SYSTEM_REFERENCE_ID it only restricts the result of
            another lookup to customers that hold an identifier of this type.
          </xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SYSTEM_REFERENCE_ID" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>
            Identifier value in the external system; exact match together with SYSTEM_REFERENCE.
            Normalisation applied by the service before matching:
              - any leading "W" / "w" characters are removed;
              - for SYSTEM_REFERENCE = LOYALTY_ID only: if the value as sent is not found, the service
                retries with the value left-padded with zeros to 8 characters, then with leading
                zeros removed.
            Supplied without SYSTEM_REFERENCE it matches nothing.
          </xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FIRST_NAME" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>
            First name. Exact match when used to narrow a phone or e-mail lookup; "contains" match in
            the ZIP_CODE lookup; general name search when used without phone / e-mail / ZIP.
          </xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LAST_NAME" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Last name. Same matching rules as FIRST_NAME.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CITY" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>
            City filter. Presence of CITY (or PROVINCE) switches the request to the address lookup;
            PHONE_NUMBER and EMAIL_ID lookups are then not performed.
          </xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="STATE" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>
            IGNORED by the loyalty-app service generation: the element is recognised but its value is
            discarded. Use PROVINCE to filter by state / province.
          </xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="PROVINCE" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>
            State / province / region code used by the address lookup. NOTE: PROVINCE does not count
            toward the "at least one filter" rule: a request whose only filter is PROVINCE returns
            E105. Combine it with CITY or another counting filter.
          </xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ZIP_CODE" type="ZipType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>ZIP / postal code lookup; see ZipType for the length rules. May be combined with FIRST_NAME and/or LAST_NAME ("contains" matches) and ZIP4.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ZIP4" type="Zip4Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>ZIP+4 extension, exact match; only used together with ZIP_CODE.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EMAIL_ID" type="EmailType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>E-mail lookup ("starts-with" on the trimmed value, current addresses only). Not performed when CITY, PROVINCE or PHONE_NUMBER is present.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="PHONE_NUMBER" type="PhoneType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Phone lookup. Not performed when CITY or PROVINCE is present. When no customer is found, deployments with phone validation enabled add a RESPONSE_DATA block describing the number to the "not found" response.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="STORE_ID" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>
            Store context. Optional: when omitted or empty the service uses the deployment's first
            configured store. When supplied it must be a known store identifier, otherwise E127
            (this check runs before the "at least one filter" check). The store selects the brand
            context used for the solicitation flags in the response; it does not filter customers.
          </xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RECORDS_LIMIT" type="RecordsLimit" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Read but not applied; results are capped at 50. See RecordsLimit.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="REQUEST_DATE" type="xs:string" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Optional client timestamp. Read but not used by the service (free text).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:all>
	</xs:complexType>
	<!-- ===== Root element ===== -->
	<xs:element name="REQUEST">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>
        Envelope for the findCustomersB request: one CRITERIA block. (The service tolerates a missing
        CRITERIA block but then has no filters and returns E105.)
      </xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexType>
			<xs:sequence>
				<xs:element name="CRITERIA" type="CriteriaType"/>
			</xs:sequence>
		</xs:complexType>
	</xs:element>
</xs:schema>
